- Nodes start at `a1` with `x` containing only `zero`
- `step_a1` transitions to `a2` and sets `x` to contain both `zero` and `one` (nondeterministic choice)
- `step_a2` transitions to `b` and sets `x` to contain only `one` (commits to `one`)
- `step_b` reads from the previous node and stores the value in `y`

The key insight is that `y(N) = one` can only be guaranteed in `step_b` if the previous node is in state `b` or `done` (where `x` contains only `one`).

